## What "hard water" actually means

Hardness is simply a measure of dissolved minerals — mostly calcium and magnesium — picked up as water moves
through rock and soil. It is measured in grains per gallon (gpg), and the scale is straightforward: under 3 gpg
is soft, 3 to 7 is moderately hard, and anything above 7 is hard. East County sits at roughly 14 to 18 gpg.

That is not a health problem. The minerals in hard water are the same ones in your multivitamin. It is a
*plumbing* problem, and an expensive one, because those minerals do not stay dissolved forever. Heat and
evaporation drop them out of solution as scale — the chalky white crust you scrape off a showerhead, and the
same material quietly coating the inside of your pipes and water heater.

## Where your water comes from

Which district serves you depends on where in East County you live, and all three deliver hard water:

- **Helix Water District** — El Cajon, La Mesa, Lemon Grove, and part of Spring Valley
- **Padre Dam Municipal Water District** — Santee, Lakeside, and the Alpine core
- **Otay Water District** — Rancho San Diego, and parts of Jamul and Spring Valley

The common thread is imported supply. San Diego County produces very little of its own water, and the Colorado
River water that makes up much of the regional supply is naturally mineral-rich. Local treatment makes it safe
and pleasant to drink; it does not soften it.

## The symptoms you are already seeing

Most homeowners recognise hard water long before they name it:

- White or greenish crust on faucets, aerators, and showerheads
- Spots and film on glassware straight out of the dishwasher
- A haze on glass shower doors that no cleaner fully removes
- Soap and shampoo that will not lather, and skin that feels dry afterwards
- Laundry that comes out stiff or dull despite plenty of detergent
- A water heater that rumbles, pops, or takes longer to recover than it used to

That last one is the expensive symptom. Scale settles on the bottom of a tank heater and insulates the burner
from the water it is meant to heat, so the burner runs longer for the same result. Left alone, it shortens the
life of the unit by years — which is why we recommend annual flushing on every tank we service, and why
[water heater repair in El Cajon](/services/water-heater/) so often turns out to be a scale story.

## Softener, conditioner, or filter?

These three get used interchangeably in advertising, and they do genuinely different things.

**A salt-based softener** removes hardness minerals through ion exchange, swapping calcium and magnesium for a
trace of sodium. It is the only one of the three that actually reduces hardness, and it remains the most
effective answer to heavy scale. It needs salt periodically and a drain connection for its regeneration cycle.

**A salt-free conditioner** does not remove minerals. It alters their structure so they are less inclined to
stick to pipes, heaters, and fixtures. Scale forms more slowly, but your water is still technically hard —
you will see less crust, though not the slippery-soft feel a true softener gives. It needs no salt and very
little maintenance, which suits some households better.

**A filter** targets something else entirely: chlorine taste and odour, sediment, and other contaminants. A
whole-house carbon filter improves how your water tastes and smells; an under-sink reverse osmosis system gives
you very clean drinking and cooking water at one tap. Neither touches hardness.

Plenty of East County homes end up with a combination — a softener or conditioner protecting the plumbing, plus
an RO tap in the kitchen. If you are weighing the options, our page on
[water filtration and softener systems](/services/water-filtration/) walks through sizing and installation.

## Hard water and tankless heaters

Tankless water heaters deserve a specific warning here, because they are increasingly popular in inland homes
and they are more scale-sensitive than tank units, not less.

A tankless heater works by pushing water through a compact heat exchanger at high temperature. That is exactly
the condition under which minerals precipitate out, so scale builds on the exchanger surfaces where it does the
most damage to efficiency. Manufacturers respond by requiring periodic descaling — and by voiding warranty
coverage when it has not been done.

None of that makes tankless a bad choice in El Cajon or Santee. It makes water treatment part of the
installation conversation rather than an afterthought. When we install a tankless unit here, we fit isolation
valves so descaling is a simple service call, and we talk through softening or conditioning ahead of the unit.
Done that way, tankless performs well for decades in hard-water territory.

## What we would do first

If hard water is on your mind, start with a number instead of a guess. A hardness test takes minutes at your
kitchen sink and tells you exactly what you are dealing with, which in turn determines what size and type of
system makes sense. We are happy to run one — we are a
[plumber serving El Cajon](/service-areas/el-cajon/) and the surrounding East County communities, and the test
costs you nothing but the appointment.
